From 43cb66f2be30873f8320a8e367aed13e6bd3a26a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 21:02:32 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 127/209] fix: a lens flare on the wrong kind of light took the
 RenderThread down

The symbolised dump (sys_dump_type 3) put the access violation in
CryRenderD3D11!CLensOpticsStage::Execute+0x711, LensOptics.cpp:67, on
`mov rcx,[rdi]` with rdi = bc7d1500`3b9741d0 - the RootOpticsElement* that line 65 read out of the
SRenderLight line 64 fetched with GetLight(eDLT_DeferredLight, pOD->m_nLightID). The pointer is not
null and not freed, it is uninitialised noise, which is what you get from a field of an SRenderLight
that was never constructed.

CRenderView::AddDeferredLight routes a light into ONE OF THREE lists - eDLT_DeferredLight,
eDLT_DeferredAmbientLight, eDLT_DeferredCubemap - and the index it returns indexes whichever list it
picked. CRenderer::EF_CheckLightMaterial stores that index on the lens-flare render object, and
CLensOpticsStage::Execute resolves it against eDLT_DeferredLight and against nothing else. A flare
on an environment probe, or on an ambient light with the switch off, therefore hands the stage an
index from a foreign index space. And m_lights is a std::list whose GetLight() walks iterators from
begin() with nothing but a CRY_ASSERT for bounds: in a Profile build an out-of-range index does not
read past the end of a buffer, it lands on the list's SENTINEL node and returns its raw memory as an
SRenderLight. One index too far is enough.

This is a stock CRYENGINE 5.7 bug and it reproduces with r_SceneReferred 0 - the same crash
signature was already recorded on 2026-09-07, before any ambient work. Routing ambient lights into
the deferred list (7e99d9ea) does not create it; it changes which lights land in which list, so it
changes how often the index falls out of range. An interior lit with ambient lights is exactly the
shape that makes the deferred list shorter than the list the index came from.

The fix keeps the index and the list together:

- CRenderView::ResolveDeferredLightList() is the routing decision extracted into one function.
  AddDeferredLight now calls it, so the answer has a single source of truth and the consumer side
  can ask the same question. Pure refactor - the same three branches, the same bSort behaviour.
- CRenderer::EF_AddDeferredLight passes the resolved list to EF_CheckLightMaterial, which no longer
  queues an EFSLIST_LENSOPTICS render item unless the light really lives in eDLT_DeferredLight with
  an index >= 0. Non-flare light materials go to EFSLIST_TRANSP_AW and never read m_nLightID, so
  they are untouched. nRenderLightID is now a RenderLightIndex rather than a uint16, so a negative
  index cannot wrap to 65535 on the way in.
- CRenderView::GetLight() bounds-checks and returns a static default-constructed SRenderLight - null
  optics element, null occlusion query - instead of the sentinel. The accessor has a dozen callers
  and should not be a crash primitive in a build with asserts compiled out.
- CLensOpticsStage::Execute verifies the index against GetLightsCount(eDLT_DeferredLight) before the
  fetch, so a mismatch shows up as a missing flare rather than as a default light.

Content effect, and it is a restoration rather than a regression because the stock behaviour here
was undefined: with Scene Referred off, a lens flare attached to an ambient light now draws nothing
- it never had a meaningful index into the deferred list. With Scene Referred on, an ambient light
IS a deferred light, so its flare works like any other. Flares on ordinary lights and on the sun are
unchanged on both paths, and no other pixel moves.

